I knew the ribosome was important but to  be honest many awards have gone to the  ribosome and but but not to me and so I  had gradually lowered my expectations of  any such award so I actually thought  initially that it was a prank call and  because I have friends who do play this  sort of prank of course it would be a  kind of cruel prank but I nevertheless  didn't really quite believe it and I  asked to actually speak to a member of  the committee whom I know because he's a  well-known ribosome scientist and when I  heard his voice then it really sank in  with a shock that it was real so huh  well I like to joke that ever since  Rutherford a knowledge of chemistry is  not a prerequisite for this whole horde  because actually I think I would flunk  an undergraduate chemistry exam so I'm  kind of tickled to here to get a  chemistry Nobel Prize  the ribosome is a large macromolecular  machine it's a molecular machine that  consists of over a million atoms its  role in all cells is to take the genetic  information encoded in DNA which is  normally in the form of a double helix  now DNA gets copied to messenger RNA and  both DNA and RNA contain a sequence of  the building blocks of nucleic acid and  that sequence specifies the sequence of  amino acids that make up a protein but  to go from the language of nucleic acids  which has four types of building block  to the language of proteins which has 20  types of amino acids requires a  translating machine and that complex  translating machine is the ribosome and  the reason that's so important is  because many of the really catalytic  activities of the cell and structures of  the cell are made up of protein  by the time I started working on it  quite a lot was known it was known that  there were two subunits the small  subunit would bind this messenger RNA  the large subunit would form the peptide  bond that antibiotics targeted the  ribosome the problem was to go from  there to a detailed understanding of the  ribosome was very difficult and that's  because the ribosome is as I told you  enormous it's over a million atoms and  people made a number of valiant efforts  over the years which really vastly  improved our understanding of the  ribosome but ultimately I think a  high-resolution structure would it was  clear that that would elevate our  understanding to a new level that's what  allows you to know where the atoms are  and unless you know where the atoms are  you don't know where the bonds are what  what's bonding with what what the  interactions are and so we can't  interpret things in chemical terms and  this is why I think the high resolution  structures really change everything the  idea of trying to get an atomic  structure of the ribosome really goes  back to our day on earth who's one of  the co winners of this Nobel Prize and  she decided to try and crystallize the  entire ribosome or ribosomal subunits  and initially her crystals weren't very  good there were a number of technical  difficulties because no one had solved a  structure nearly as big as a ribosome it  was a tremendous feeling because to  actually see you know these proteins and  large strands of RNA in the electron  density meant we'd crack the problem and  then it was a question of just improving  the resolution to the point where we  could ex  interpreted in atomic terms that was a  thrilling period if you think of these  crystal structures they're like  snapshots of a particular state of the  molecule and as you know from looking at  any machine like a car engine machines  move to consume energy to do all sorts  of things and so if you want to  understand how the ribosome works you  need to get that snapshot at different  stages of the process and that's still  an ongoing work in many different labs  and that's what we'd like but of course  that sort of snapshot isn't really going  to tell us everything or even much of  the story either I think the ultimate  story will come from a combined effort  from biochemists geneticists people who  look at single molecule techniques so  all these techniques are going to help  unravel exactly how the ribosome works  that's going to take many years but I  think knowing what the fundamental  architecture is in molecular terms makes  all of this other work much more  powerful  so I'm kind of dismayed to see this this  constant emphasis on translational  research drowning out the idea that it's  our basic research that provides the  seed corn from which we can have  translational research without these  kinds of fundamental discoveries and I  should tell you the ribosome right after  the structures were determined has  already been started to be used for  antibiotic design but that's not why we  did it okay and many discoveries like  DNA sequencing monoclonal antibodies  they're all billion-dollar industries  now and so you know you should have both  but there has to be the recognition that  really fundamental silent research pays  for itself many many times over in terms  of eventual discoveries and technology  and if you support that foundation the  rest will follow I think you simply  cannot do science in order to get a  Nobel Prize that's that's a route to  failure and unhappiness okay I think  people should pursue science because  they're interested in it that you pick a  problem that they love that they care  about that they're dying to know the  answer to and then the prizes or the  lack of prizes simply doesn't matter if  the Nobel Prize didn't exist we would  all still be very happy in fact we will  be happier because of this limitation of  three and excluding really important  contributions so we'd actually be  happier in some sense if these prizes  didn't exist and we pursued knowledge  for its own sake
 

